hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the lendau gambit  trap i'm calling it the trap even though  it's an opening only because  your opponent essentially is falling for  a trap before you get to the game but  once you have the gambit it's an 80  plus win rate but we'll go ahead and get  into it and show you how it works so it  starts out with e4 and then we have the  karo khan with c6 we play d4 hey if they  don't want to counter the center we're  going to go ahead and take control of it  with e4 and then e5  usually they're just going to strike the  center that's the whole reason you play  c6 here is then to push forward with d5  maybe exchange but not give up one of  your central pawns here then we're gonna  play the tricky move bishop d3 now a lot  of people are just going to take on e4  that's fine we're going to exchange with  our bishop and be in a fine spot so if  you see that don't worry don't feel like  you're in a terrible situation but this  gambit is going to be looking at if they  play  knight f6 not the best move from them  but does seem logical  because yeah we could push forward e5  but then they just say hey we can we can  move our knights potentially coming to  e4 if they haven't studied it all that  often or they may just not recognize all  the threats that are coming in play that  we're going to be going over in this  video  but once they play f6 we're gonna play  e5  knight e4 not really a great option  because we have f3 and once we play f3  the knight's pretty much trapped it  can't come to g5 it's gonna fall to our  bishop it can't come back to f6 or d6  that's covered by our pawn also can't  come over here to c5 because of our  other pawn and you can see it's pretty  easy we have a couple options to take if  it comes to c3 so  knight 2 e4  not really an option so where does black  go well they could just come back to g8  that's one option another option is  coming down to  g4  you typically don't want to move your  knights multiple  times in the beginning of the game now  we could play h3 and then what do they  do they can't come back to  f6 here so they come back to h6 so  they've moved their knight three times  and it ends up on h6 not exactly where  they want to go and then we play knight  to f3 we castle on the king side you can  see we have strong central control or  just much better in all facets of the  game and they have a knight that's not  going to be involved in the game  anytime soon  so the most logical move if you're just  thinking about where a human might go is  the move knight to d7 it brings it back  towards the center of the board so it's  not over here on the h file  and it's also attacking the pawn yes and  knight's not going to just take the pawn  right away it is defended by the pawn on  d4 but this seems somewhat logical  and this is where  the landau game it comes into play  because we play pawn e 6 giving up this  pawn of material now as you'll see they  can't actually take the pawn and accept  the game but if they do  they automatically lose but this is why  i'm calling it a trap because they  essentially have to fall for the trap  before we can actually play the landau  gamut so when we play this e6 we're  hoping that they accept it if they  accept on e6 then we play queen h5 check  the only option they have to stop it is  g6 and then we have two ways to  checkmate one is queen takes on g6 and  they take and then we play bishop on  g6 checkmate the other option you can  probably guess we can  take with our bishop first  and then they take and then we recapture  but then you have to live with yourself  knowing that you could have sacrificed  your queen  and you didn't so the only logical  option is to sacrifice the queen they  take and then you take with your bishop  this is kind of a cool checkmate here  with bishop on g6 they have no way to  defend it with their knight on  d7 here  but knowing that they can't actually  take our pawn  then what did they end up doing okay  well they they need to go ahead and move  their knight it's under attack it would  wouldn't make a lot of sense if they did  not so they have a couple options they  could play knight to b6 and they could  play knight to  f6 for both options doesn't matter if  they come knight f6 or to b6 in both  scenarios we're going to take on f7  and check the king now the king should  always take the pawn on f7 if they don't  they're just going to be in a worse spot  and we're going to do the exact same  thing because we're playing knight to f3  either way the difference is  if they don't take our pawn they have to  worry about this pawn on f7 it's  blocking a lot of critical squares any  of the pieces coming here to e8 or g8  can't do that  and they still have to worry about the  knight coming to e5 checking the king  the king is also blocking out the light  square bishops that's just an absolutely  terrible move so the only other option  is to take on f7 but then we play knight  to f3 you can see the knight can easily  get involved it has an outpost on e5  checking the king can also come to g5 it  wants to check in the king  white's pr in a pretty safe spot and yes  there was a gambit  but they couldn't accept it and so we're  not down material in this gambit pretty  easy win for white we can castle on the  king side we can get our rook our queen  on the e file double up on the semi-open  file put a lot of pressure on our  opponent get our dark square bishop  involved into the game their king is in  no man's land they also can't get their  knight involved into the game because  they are playing the carl con why  they're doing that who knows so knight  to c6 is not an option they're really  going to be looking to play knight to a6  and then get it involved maybe to b4  because if they play knight to d7 right  away it does block off the light square  bishop they're in a world of hurt and  that's why if you actually get to play  this gambit you are going to have an  extremely high win percentage 80 plus  percent so that is the lambo gambit trap  definitely a fun little thing to try out  if you run up against the cairo khan  sometimes try that bishop to d3 and just  see what they do remember even if they  don't play knight two f6 you're not in a  bad spot you can always just take with  your bishop and you have a fine opening  here but if you ever do run into the  knight to f6  you have a pretty good chance of running  this trap on your opponent hopefully you  guys enjoyed this video thank you so  much for watching  let me know in the comments below if you  like these type of videos but thanks  again i'll see you in the next video  you

